[Event "Polerio Defence: the main line"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/NMtH8AT9/TsSduqSz"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Welcome to the Polerio defence: when his knight and bishop gang up on your f pawn, you let him take a pawn, then chase his pieces around the board while yours come out. }
1. e4 { He opens with his king pawn; his queen pawn to d4 would be another study. Meet it head on: your king pawn goes two squares as well. [%cal Ye7e5] } 1... e5 { Your pawn takes its share of the middle. If his bishop comes out before his knight, answer with your king knight to f6. } (1... c5 { The Sicilian: just as good, and it has a study of its own. } 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 { About level, but a different battle, with his bishop hitting your knight. Today your king pawn meets his head on. [%csl Bc5] }) (1... e6 { The French: sound too, and another study. It shuts your light bishop in behind the pawn. } 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 { About level: a slow, closed game, and today we want open lines. [%csl Be6] }) (1... c6 { The Caro-Kann: just as good, solid and slow. } 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 { Level: a calm fight for the centre, and not the one we are practising. [%csl Bc6] }) (1... d5 { The Scandinavian: you hit his pawn at once, but your pieces come out a little behind. } 2. exd5 Nf6 3. Nf3 Nxd5 { About level, though he gets a free hand in the centre. Push your king pawn two squares instead. [%csl Bd5] }) (1... d6 { The Pirc family: flexible, but it hands him the whole middle. } 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 { About level: he built a big centre, and that is another opening. [%csl Bd6] }) (1... Nc6 { A rare knight move: playable, but it blocks your c pawn. } 2. d4 d5 { About level: his pawns take the centre, and it is a different game. [%csl Bc6] }) 2. Nf3 { His knight attacks your pawn on e5. Guard it and develop at the same time: queen knight to c6. [%cal Yb8c6] } 2... Nc6 { The knight guards your pawn and joins the game. His bishop to b5 would be the Spanish, and pawn to d4 the Scotch: both are studies of their own. } (2... Nf6 { The Petrov: also sound. After his knight takes e5, knight to c6 would be the Stafford gambit, which has its own study. } 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 { About level: the Petrov, a calm opening of its own. [%csl Bf6] }) (2... d6 { The Philidor: also sound. The pawn guards e5 instead of a knight, and you get a quieter game. [%cal Gd6e5] } 3. d4 Nf6 4. Nc3 { About level, though his pieces come out a little faster. Today the knight guards the pawn. [%csl Bd6] }) 3. Bc4 { The Italian bishop stares at f7, the square only your king guards. Bring out your king knight and hit his pawn on e4. [%cal Yg8f6][%csl Rf7] } 3... Nf6 { The two knights defence: your knight hits his king pawn straight away. If he guards it with pawn to d3 or knight to c3, or castles, bring your king bishop out and castle: that quiet Italian is another study. } (3... Bc5 { The Italian: just as good. Your bishop stares at f2 while his stares at f7. } 4. c3 Nf6 5. d3 { Level: the quiet Italian, which has a study of its own. Today your knight comes out first. [%csl Bc5] }) (3... h6 { The pawn stops a knight jump to g5, but it spends a move on the edge of the board. } 4. d4 exd4 5. Nxd4 Nf6 { Slightly better for him: he took the centre while your pawn on h6 did nothing for your pieces. [%csl Rh6] }) (3... Be7 { The Hungarian: also sound, and very solid. } 4. d4 exd4 5. Nxd4 { About level: a sturdy but passive game, and not the one we are practising. [%csl Be7] }) (3... d6 { Also sound: the pawn guards e5 and opens a road for your light bishop. [%cal Gd6e5] } 4. d4 { About level, a calm game. Today your knight hits his pawn instead. [%csl Bd6] }) (3... Nd4 { This sets a trap for a greedy knight, but he does not have to take the bait. } 4. Nxd4 exd4 5. O-O { Better for him: the knights came off, and your pawn on d4 is weak and far from home. [%csl Rd4] }) 4. Ng5 { Now his knight jumps in too: knight and bishop both hit your f pawn. Shut the bishop out with your queen pawn, two squares. Had he pushed his own queen pawn to d4 instead, you would take it: that is the Scotch gambit. [%cal Yd7d5,Rg5f7,Rc4f7] } 4... d5 { Your pawn cuts the bishop off from f7 and attacks his king pawn. } (4... Bc5 { The Traxler: you ignore f7 and hit f2 instead. Wild, and risky. } 5. Nxf7 Bxf2+ 6. Kf1 Qe7 7. Nxh8 { About level, but only if your attack works: his knight took your rook in the corner, and his king is on the run. [%csl Rh8] }) (4... Nxe4 { You grab his pawn, but f7 falls with check. } 5. Bxf7+ Ke7 6. d4 h6 7. Nxe4 Kxf7 { Better for him: the material is level, but your king is out in the open and can never castle. [%csl Rf7] }) (4... Qe7 { The queen guards f7, but his bishop takes it anyway, with check. [%cal Ge7f7,Rc4f7] } 5. Bxf7+ Kd8 6. Bb3 { Better for him: a clean pawn up, and your king has lost the right to castle. [%csl Rd8] }) (4... h6 { Kicking the knight looks natural, but it just takes on f7. } 5. Nxf7 Qe7 6. Nxh8 { Lost for you: his knight forked your queen and rook, and the rook in the corner is gone. [%csl Rh8] }) 5. exd5 { He takes, and his pawn on d5 now hits your knight. Do not take back: that walks into the fried liver, his knight sacrifice on f7. Jump to the edge and hit his bishop instead. [%cal Yc6a5] } 5... Na5 { This is the Polerio move: the knight looks silly on the edge, but it chases his bishop, and every move he spends running is a move you spend developing. } (5... Nxd5 { The natural recapture, and it walks into the fried liver. } 6. Nxf7 Kxf7 7. Qf3+ Ke6 8. Nc3 { About level on paper, but dangerous: your king was dragged out to e6, and his pieces pile onto the pinned knight on d5. [%csl Re6,Rd5] }) (5... Nd4 { The Fritz: the knight jumps forward and the game turns wild. } 6. c3 b5 7. Bf1 { About level, but a maze of loose pieces: his pawn hits your knight, and your b pawn is offered as well. [%csl Rd4] }) (5... b5 { The Ulvestad: a pawn offered to knock his bishop off its diagonal. } 6. Bf1 Nd4 7. c3 { About level: a wild gambit where pieces hang on both sides. Today we play the calmer knight move to the edge. [%csl Bb5] }) 6. Bb5+ { Check, and his bishop escapes. Block with your c pawn: it chases the bishop once more. Had he played pawn to d3 instead, you would first kick his knight with pawn to h6. [%cal Yc7c6] } 6... c6 { The pawn blocks the check and attacks the bishop at the same time. } (6... Bd7 { Just as good: the bishop blocks the check. } 7. Qe2 { Level, a quieter game: his queen hits your pawn on e5. The c pawn move is ours because it chases his bishop. [%csl Bd7] }) (6... Nd7 { The knight blocks the check, but it shuts your own bishop in. } 7. Ne6 fxe6 8. dxe6 { Better for him: his pawn on e6 hits your knight, and your king side is torn open. [%csl Re6] }) 7. dxc6 { He takes, and his pawn on c6 now hits b7. Take back with your b pawn. [%cal Yb7c6] } 7... bxc6 { Your pawn on c6 hits his bishop again, and the b column opens for your rook. You are a pawn down now: that is the price of the Polerio, and time is what it buys. } (7... Nxc6 { Just as good: the knight comes back to the middle. } 8. O-O h6 9. Nf3 { About level, but his bishop on b5 pins your knight, so you must untangle first. The b pawn recapture keeps your knight free. [%csl Bc6] }) 8. Be2 { His bishop runs home to e2: that is its third move. Now chase the knight: pawn to h6. [%cal Yh7h6] } 8... h6 { The pawn kicks the knight on g5. It has to move again. } (8... Bc5 { Natural development, but it leaves his knight on g5 in peace. } 9. d3 h6 10. Ne4 Nxe4 11. dxe4 { Slightly better for him: a pair of knights came off, and you are simply a pawn down. [%csl Re4] }) (8... Be7 { Solid, but his knight on g5 is left alone. } 9. d3 O-O 10. O-O { Slightly better for him: both kings are castled, and you are a pawn down with nothing chased. [%csl Rg5] }) (8... Nd5 { The knight heads for f4, but he answers calmly. } 9. d3 h6 10. Nf3 { Slightly better for him: his knight stepped back to f3 when it chose to, and your pawns no longer chase it. [%csl Rf3] }) (8... Bd6 { The bishop comes out, but his knight keeps its post on g5. } 9. d3 Bc7 10. O-O O-O { Slightly better for him: he castled in comfort and keeps the extra pawn. [%csl Rg5] }) (8... Bf5 { The bishop develops, but the knight on g5 is never asked to move. } 9. d3 h6 10. Nf3 { Slightly better for him: he retreated in his own time, a pawn up. [%csl Rf3] }) 9. Nf3 { The knight steps back. Chase it again with your king pawn. Had it gone to h3 instead, your bishop would come to c5 and you would castle. [%cal Ye5e4] } 9... e4 { Your pawn hits the knight a second time and grabs space in the middle. } 10. Ne5 { The knight jumps to e5, its only good square. Hit it with your bishop from d6. [%cal Yf8d6] } 10... Bd6 { Your bishop comes out with a threat: it attacks the knight on e5. } (10... Bc5 { Just as good: the bishop aims at f2 instead of d6. } 11. O-O { Level: he castles at once, and your bishop points at his king. [%csl Bc5] }) (10... Qc7 { Just as good: the queen comes first and the bishop follows. } 11. d4 exd3 12. Nxd3 Bd6 { Level: the same position as our main line, with queen and bishop on the diagonal to h2. [%csl Bc7] }) (10... Qd4 { Just as good: your queen hits the knight on e5 and his f pawn at once. } 11. f4 Bc5 12. Rf1 { Level, and sharp: his rook had to leave h1 to guard f2, so he can no longer castle on the king side. [%csl Rf1][%cal Rf1f2] }) (10... Qd5 { The queen hits the knight, but in the middle she is a target. } 11. d4 exd3 12. Nxd3 Bd6 13. O-O { Better for him: he castled first, and your queen on d5 stands in the open. [%csl Rd5] }) (10... Bf5 { The bishop develops, but it does not hit the knight, so he gets a free move. } 11. d4 Bd6 12. Nc3 { About level, but his knight on e5 is guarded now and his other knight came out for free. [%csl Rc3] }) 11. d4 { He guards the knight with his queen pawn, two squares, right past your pawn on e4. Take it en passant, as if it had moved one square. [%cal Ye4d3] } 11... exd3 { Taken en passant: his centre pawns are gone, and he has to spend another move taking back. } (11... O-O { Just as good: you castle first and leave his pawn alone. } 12. O-O { Level: both kings are safe, and your pawn on e4 still cramps him. [%csl Be4] }) (11... Qc7 { The queen joins the bishop, but his pawn on d4 now holds his knight firmly. } 12. Nc3 O-O 13. O-O { About level: his centre stands firm. Taking en passant kept the lines open. [%csl Rd4] }) (11... Bxe5 { You give up your good bishop for his knight, and his pawn takes back with a threat. } 12. dxe5 Qxd1+ 13. Bxd1 Nd5 { Better for him: the queens are gone, and you are a pawn down with nothing left to chase. [%csl Re5] }) 12. Nxd3 { His knight takes back on d3, its fifth move of the game. Now line up your queen behind the bishop, on the diagonal to h2. [%cal Yd8c7,Gd6h2] } 12... Qc7 { Queen and bishop now aim together at his pawn on h2, and he has to look after it. [%cal Rd6h2] } (12... O-O { Just as good: you castle first and bring the queen later. } 13. O-O Qc7 { Level: queen and bishop join up on the diagonal to his king all the same. [%cal Gc7h2] }) (12... Bf5 { Just as good: the bishop develops and eyes his knight on d3. } 13. b3 { Level: your bishops on d6 and f5 are both active. [%csl Bf5] }) (12... Rb8 { Also sound: the rook takes the open b column. } 13. b3 { Level: your rook takes the open b column, and his b pawn steps up to meet it. [%csl Bb8] }) (12... c5 { Also sound: the pawn grabs space on the queen side. } 13. Bf3 Rb8 { Level, if a little loose: his bishop on f3 eyes your rook in the corner, so it steps to b8. [%csl Bc5] }) (12... Nd5 { Also playable: the knight heads for the middle. } 13. b3 O-O 14. Bb2 { About level: his bishop takes the long diagonal before you get your attack going. [%csl Bd5] }) 13. h3 { His h pawn steps up one square, out of the bishop's reach. Against b3, g3 or a knight move you would castle all the same. Now tuck your king away. [%cal Ye8g8] } 13... O-O { Castled. You are a pawn down, but count: four of your pieces are out and your king is safe, against his two, with his queen bishop and knight still at home. That is the Polerio: a pawn for time. } (13... Bf5 { Just as good: the bishop develops before you castle. } 14. Bg4 Qe7+ 15. Kf1 { Level: your check made his king step to f1, and now he cannot castle. [%csl Rf1] }) (13... c5 { Also sound: your pawn grabs space first. } 14. Na3 Rb8 { Level: your rook takes the open b column, and castling can wait a move. [%csl Bc5] }) 0-1


[Event "Polerio Defence: the bishop retreats to a4"]
[Result "0-1"]
