[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/p4ppp/2p2n2/nB2p1N1/8/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R_w_KQkq_-_0_8"]

{ Your b pawn has just taken back on c6, and it attacks his bishop on b5 once more. }
8. Ba4 { His bishop steps back to a4. Club players choose it more than any other move, but it is a mistake: the bishop ends up far from home and his king short of defenders. How do you start? } 8... h6 { Right: the pawn kicks his knight, and your e pawn is ready to hit it again if it lands on f3. } (8... Bc5 { The bishop develops, but his knight on g5 is left alone. } 9. Nc3 Bg4 10. f3 { About level: his knight kept its post and he has time to sort himself out. [%csl Rg5] }) (8... Be7 { Solid, but your pawns stop chasing. } 9. d3 h6 10. Nf3 { Slightly better for him: his knight went home to f3 in its own time. [%csl Rf3] }) (8... Nd5 { The knight heads for the middle, but his pieces are not chased. } 9. d3 Nb6 10. Bb3 { Slightly better for him: his bishop found a safe spot, and your knights are tangled. [%csl Rb3] }) (8... Bd7 { The bishop steps out of the way, but that chases nothing. } 9. d3 h6 10. Ne4 Nxe4 11. dxe4 { Slightly better for him: a pair of knights was swapped, his pawn now stands on e4, and you are a pawn down. [%csl Re4] }) (8... Ba6 { The bishop stops him castling, but his queen jumps to f3 and eyes your pawn on c6. } 9. Qf3 Qc7 10. Nc3 { Slightly better for him: his pieces came out, and nothing of his was chased. [%csl Rf3] }) (8... Bg4 { The bishop hits his queen, but a pawn to f3 blocks it and sends it back. } 9. f3 Bc8 10. d3 { About level: your bishop went there and back while he built a wall. [%csl Rf3] }) (8... Bd6 { The bishop develops, but his knight on g5 stays put. } 9. Nc3 O-O 10. O-O { Slightly better for him: both kings castled, and his knight on g5 never had to move. [%csl Rg5] }) (8... Bf5 { The bishop comes out, but his queen steps to e2 and hits your pawn on e5. } 9. Qe2 Nd7 { Slightly better for him: your knight had to go back to guard the pawn. [%csl Re5] }) (8... Bb7 { The bishop takes the long diagonal, but it only stares at your own pawn on c6. [%cal Rb7c6] } 9. d3 h6 10. Nf3 { Better for him: his knight went home in its own time, and your bishop is shut in. [%csl Rb7] }) (8... e4 { The pawn push comes too early, with his knight still on g5. } 9. O-O Bd6 10. d3 { Better for him: your pawn on e4 is loose, and his king is safe. [%csl Re4] }) (8... Qc7 { The queen guards e5, but she does nothing to his knight on g5. [%cal Gc7e5] } 9. Nc3 h6 10. Nge4 Nxe4 11. Nxe4 { Slightly better for him: a pair of knights came off, and his other knight now sits on e4. [%csl Re4] }) (8... Bb4 { The bishop comes out, but his c pawn sends it straight back. } 9. c3 Bd6 { Slightly better for him: your bishop spent two moves to reach d6. [%csl Rd6] }) (8... Be6 { The bishop develops, but his knight on g5 swaps it off. } 9. Nxe6 fxe6 10. O-O { Much better for him: your pawns are wrecked, and his king is safe. [%csl Re6] }) 9. Nf3 { The knight goes back to f3. Keep chasing it. } 9... e4 { Your pawn hits the knight again and takes space in the middle. If it runs home to g1, bring your bishop to c5. } (9... Ba6 { The bishop stops him castling, but it lets his knight rest on f3. } 10. d3 e4 11. Nfd2 { About level: his knight stepped back safely, and the chance to hit it at once is gone. [%csl Rd2] }) (9... Bc5 { Natural development, but the knight on f3 is left in peace. } 10. O-O Ba6 11. Re1 { About level: he castled, and his knight was never chased. [%csl Rf3] }) (9... Bd6 { The bishop guards e5, which the pawn push made unnecessary. [%cal Gd6e5] } 10. d3 O-O 11. O-O { About level: he castled, and your lead in time slipped away. [%csl Rf3] }) (9... Qc7 { The queen guards e5, but it is slower than pushing the pawn. [%cal Gc7e5] } 10. d3 Bd6 11. O-O { Slightly better for him: he castled, and his knight was never chased. [%csl Rf3] }) (9... Be6 { The bishop develops, but his knight on f3 is left in peace. } 10. c3 e4 11. Nd4 { About level: your pawn chased the knight a move late, and it found d4. [%csl Rd4] }) (9... Be7 { Too slow: he gets his pawn to d3 first. } 10. d3 e4 11. dxe4 Qxd1+ 12. Kxd1 Nxe4 { Slightly better for him: the queens came off, and you are a pawn down in a quiet game. [%csl Re4] }) (9... Bg4 { The bishop pins his knight to his queen, but his pawn to h3 asks it to decide. } 10. d3 Bd6 11. h3 Bh5 { About level: the pin holds, but his knight was never chased. [%csl Rf3] }) (9... Bf5 { The bishop develops, but his pawn to d4 strikes at your centre. } 10. d4 exd4 11. O-O { Better for him: he opened the centre and castled while your king still waits. [%csl Re8] }) (9... Bb7 { The bishop takes a diagonal your own c pawn blocks. } 10. d3 Bd6 11. O-O { Better for him: he castled, and your bishop bites on your own pawn. [%csl Rb7] }) (9... Bb4 { The bishop comes out, but his a pawn sends it back. } 10. a3 Bd6 { Slightly better for him: your bishop took two moves, and his knight was never chased. [%csl Rf3] }) (9... Bd7 { The bishop comes to d7, and nothing guards your pawn on e5. } 10. Nxe5 { Better for him: his knight took your e pawn, and he is two pawns up. [%csl Re5] }) 10. Qe2 { His queen steps onto the e column, and now your pawn on e4 is pinned to your king: it cannot take the knight. What now? } 10... Be6 { The bishop steps between queen and king, so your pawn is free again, and his knight is hit once more. } (10... Bd6 { Also strong: the bishop comes out, and castling will free your pawn. } 11. Nc3 O-O 12. Nd4 { Better for you, a touch less than the bishop move to e6: his knight found a square in the middle. [%csl Rd4] }) (10... Bc5 { Also strong: the bishop hits f2, but your pawn stays pinned for now. } 11. d3 Ba6 12. O-O { Better for you, though he got time to castle. [%csl Rg1] }) (10... Be7 { This breaks the pin too, but the bishop on e7 becomes a target. } 11. Ne5 O-O 12. O-O { Slightly better for you, but most of your lead is gone: with your bishop on e7, the queen fork on d4 no longer works. [%csl Re7] }) (10... Qe7 { The queen breaks the pin, but she blocks your own bishop. } 11. Ng1 Qd8 12. Nc3 { Level: your queen went there and back, and your lead is gone. [%csl Rd8] }) (10... Bg4 { The bishop hits nothing important, and he breaks your pawn chain. } 11. d3 Bd6 12. dxe4 { Slightly better for him: he won back time and your pawn on e4 is gone. [%csl Re4] }) (10... Bb4 { The bishop comes out, but it does not break the pin on your pawn. } 11. O-O O-O { Better for you, but less: he castled, and his knight on f3 is safe for now. [%csl Rf3] }) (10... Bf5 { The bishop guards your pawn, but the pin down the e column stays. } 11. d3 Bc5 12. dxe4 Nxe4 { Level: the pawns came off, and your big lead is gone. [%csl Re4] }) (10... Bb7 { The bishop cannot break the pin, and his pawn to d3 attacks your chain. } 11. d3 Be7 12. dxe4 { Better for him: he won your pawn on e4 and is two pawns up. [%csl Re4] }) (10... Qb6 { The queen eyes f2, but the pin on your pawn stays. } 11. Nc3 Ba6 12. d3 { About level, a long way short of what the bishop move to e6 gets. [%csl Ge6] }) (10... Bd7 { The bishop does not block the e column, so the pin stays. } 11. d3 Qe7 12. dxe4 Qxe4 13. Qxe4+ Nxe4 { Better for him: the queens came off, and you are still a pawn down in a worse game. [%csl Rd7] }) (10... e3 { The pawn runs forward, but it only gets in the way. } 11. Nc3 Rb8 12. d3 { Much better for him: your pawn on e3 is loose, and his pieces are coming out. [%csl Re3] }) (10... Ba6 { The bishop hits his queen, but she simply takes it, and the swaps that follow go his way. } 11. Qxa6 exf3 12. Bxc6+ Nxc6 13. Qxc6+ Nd7 14. Qxf3 { Much better for him: he came out of the swaps three pawns up. [%csl Rf3] }) 11. Ng1 { The knight runs all the way home. Two other squares were worse: on e5 your queen could hit the knight and the bishop together, and on d4 the knight would simply hang to her. Now bring another piece out with a threat. } 11... Bc5 { The bishop comes out and aims at f2, next to his king. } (11... Bd6 { Also good: the bishop develops, but it aims at nothing near his king. } 12. b3 { Better for you, but his bishop is about to come out, and the pressure eases. [%csl Rb3] }) (11... Be7 { Too modest: the bishop hits nothing from e7. } 12. b3 O-O 13. Nc3 { Better for you, but less so: his pieces are finding squares. [%csl Rc3] }) (11... Qd4 { The queen forks nothing now, and his bishop steps back to b3. } 12. Bb3 Nxb3 13. axb3 { About level: the pieces came off, his king is safe enough, and most of your lead is gone. [%csl Rb3] }) (11... Qb6 { The queen hits f2, but your bishop could have done that job. } 12. d4 Bd6 13. Bb3 { Better for you, but his pawn on d4 blocked the road to f2. [%csl Rd4] }) (11... Bb4 { The bishop hits nothing on b4. } 12. b3 O-O 13. Bb2 { Better for you, but his bishop came out, and your pressure on f2 never started. [%csl Gf2] }) (11... e3 { Pushing the pawn to e3 only gives it away to his f pawn. } 12. fxe3 Bb4 { Better for him: your pawn on e3 is gone, and you are two pawns down. [%csl Re3] }) 12. b3 { He pushes his b pawn to let his bishop out on the long diagonal; pawn to d3 would get the same answer. Your king is still in the middle, and his queen sits on the e column. [%cal Rc1b2] } 12... O-O { Castled. Count the pieces: four of yours are out and your king is safe, while only his queen and bishop are out and his king is stuck in the middle. That is the Polerio: a pawn for time. } (12... Rb8 { The rook takes the b column, but your king waits on the e column. } 13. Ba3 Bxa3 14. Nxa3 { Better for you, but less so: he swapped off your good bishop while your king waited. [%csl Re8] }) (12... Qd4 { The queen swings over, but his knight comes out and your queen gets pushed around. } 13. Nc3 O-O 14. Qe3 { Better for you, but his queen came to e3 to offer a swap. [%csl Re3] }) (12... Bf5 { The bishop develops, but your king is left behind. } 13. Bb2 O-O 14. Nc3 { Better for you, though he got his bishop out to the long diagonal. [%csl Rb2] }) (12... Ng4 { The knight jumps at f2, but his knight comes to h3 and guards it. } 13. Nh3 Qf6 { Better for you, but his king side holds for now. [%csl Rh3] }) (12... Bc4 { The bishop hits his queen, but his pawn simply takes it. } 13. bxc4 { Much better for him: your bishop is gone for nothing. [%csl Rc4] }) (12... e3 { The pawn lunges forward, but his f pawn simply takes it. } 13. fxe3 O-O 14. Nf3 { Better for him: your pawn is gone, and his knight came back into play. [%csl Re3] }) (12... Qc7 { The queen lines up, but his bishop comes out to the long diagonal. [%cal Rc1b2] } 13. Bb2 O-O-O 14. Bxf6 gxf6 { Better for you, but his bishop took your knight and your king side is torn. [%csl Rf6] }) 0-1


[Event "Polerio Defence: the queen comes to f3"]
