 your lead is gone. [%csl Rb7] }) (10... c5 { The pawn grabs space, but it gives him time to open the centre. } 11. d3 exd3 12. Qxd3 { About level: his queen came out, and your lead is gone. [%csl Rd3] }) (10... Bg4 { The bishop steps to g4, where his bishop takes it, and taking back drops your knight to his queen. } 11. Bxg4 { Lost for you: a whole bishop down. [%csl Rg4] }) (10... Ba6 { The bishop offers a trade, and his bishop takes it for nothing. } 11. Bxa6 { Lost for you: your bishop is gone, and a6 had no guard. [%csl Ra6] }) 11. d3 { He opens a road for his other bishop, but that is slow: pawn to d4 was his best, and pawn to c3 would be no better than this. Now pile up on the weak pawn next to his king. } 11... Qb6 { Queen and bishop both aim at f2, and only his king defends it. } (11... Qd4 { Just as good: from d4 the queen hits f2 and his b pawn. } 12. Be3 Qxb2 13. Nd2 Bxe3 14. fxe3 { Much the same: you took his b pawn, and his king is still stuck in the middle. [%csl Gb2] }) (11... O-O { Natural, but it gives him time to cover f2. [%cal Gc5f2] } 12. Nc3 Re8 13. Be3 { Slightly better for you, but his bishop blocked your attack on f2. [%csl Re3] }) (11... exd3 { You open the centre, but his queen takes back with a threat. } 12. Qxd3 Qb6 13. Kf1 { Slightly better for you, but his queen came into play for free. [%csl Rd3] }) (11... Qe7 { The queen guards e4, but it does not add to the attack on f2. [%cal Ge7e4] } 12. Nc3 O-O 13. Be3 { Slightly better for you, but his bishop on e3 closed the road to f2. [%csl Re3] }) (11... Bf5 { The bishop develops, but it adds nothing to the attack on f2. } 12. Nc3 Qe7 13. Nh3 { About level: his knight reached h3 and guards f2. [%cal Rh3f2] }) (11... Be6 { The bishop develops, but it gives his knight time to reach h3. } 12. Nh3 Bxh3 13. gxh3 { Level: you swapped your bishop for his knight, and f2 is safe. [%csl Rf2] }) (11... Bg4 { The bishop hits his bishop, but it is taken, and your knight cannot take back without dropping to his queen. } 12. Bxg4 Qd4 13. Nh3 { Much better for him: a whole bishop up, and his knight covers f2. [%cal Rh3f2] }) (11... e3 { The pawn lunges forward, and his f pawn takes it. } 12. fxe3 O-O 13. Nf3 { Better for him: two pawns up, and no pawn left on f2 for you to hit. [%csl Re3] }) (11... Bd7 { The bishop comes to d7, a quiet move that lets his knight reach h3. } 12. Nh3 Bxh3 13. gxh3 { Level: the knight is gone, but so is your attack on f2. [%csl Rf2] }) (11... Ba6 { The bishop eyes his d pawn, but f2 matters more. } 12. Nc3 { Level: his knight came out, and the chance on f2 passed. [%csl Rc3] }) (11... Bb7 { The bishop comes to b7 and bites on your own pawn. } 12. Nc3 { About level: your bishop bites on your own pawn, and his knight is out. [%csl Rb7] }) 12. Nc3 { His knight comes out and hits your pawn on e4, but f2 still has only his king to guard it. Had he grabbed your pawn instead, your knight would take back and join in. What now? } 12... Bxf2+ { Check, and the pawn is back: material is level. His king has one safe square, f1, and can never castle from there, and his king knight and queen bishop still sit at home. The Polerio paid out: time first, then the pawn. } (12... Nb7 { The knight comes back into play, but he blocks your bishop. } 13. Be3 Bxe3 14. fxe3 { Slightly better for you, but the chance to take f2 has gone. [%csl Re3] }) (12... O-O { Castling first gives him a move to cover f2. [%cal Gc5f2] } 13. Nh3 { Slightly better for you, but his knight on h3 now guards f2. [%cal Rh3f2] }) (12... exd3 { Opening the centre first gives him time. } 13. Bxd3 Bxf2+ 14. Kf1 { Slightly better for you, but his bishop came to d3 for free. [%csl Rd3] }) (12... Be6 { The bishop develops, but his knight gets to h3 and guards f2. [%cal Rg1h3,Rh3f2] } 13. Nh3 { Level: f2 has a guard now, and the chance is gone. [%csl Rh3] }) (12... Bg4 { The bishop hits his bishop, but it is simply taken. } 13. Bxg4 Bxf2+ 14. Kf1 { Much better for him: you won a pawn on f2, but he won a whole bishop. [%csl Rg4] }) (12... e3 { The pawn push opens nothing: his pawn takes it. } 13. fxe3 Bxe3 14. Bxe3 Qxe3 { Better for him: the bishops came off, and your attack on f2 is gone. [%csl Rf2] }) (12... Bf5 { The bishop comes to f5, but his knight gets to h3 first. } 13. Nh3 { Level: f2 is covered now, and the chance has passed. [%cal Rh3f2] }) (12... Bd7 { The bishop develops, but his knight reaches h3 in time. } 13. Nh3 { Level: his knight covers f2, and your chance is gone. [%cal Rh3f2] }) (12... Ba6 { The bishop eyes his d pawn instead of taking on f2. } 13. Nh3 { Level: his knight now covers f2. [%cal Rh3f2] }) (12... Bb7 { The bishop takes a blocked diagonal, and f2 slips away. } 13. Nh3 exd3 14. Qxd3 { Slightly better for him: his knight guards f2, and his queen came out. [%cal Rh3f2] }) 0-1


[Event "Polerio Defence: the knight backs off to c4"]
