{ The main line: your bishop on d6 has just hit his knight on e5, and he has to decide how to deal with it. }
11. Nc4 { His knight backs off to c4 and hits your bishop. That costs him time: pawn to d4 was his best. How do you make the most of it? [%csl Rd6] } 11... Nxc4 { Right: your knight takes his, and his bishop must take back. Your knight on the edge has done its job, and his bishop is about to make its fourth move. } (11... O-O { Castling looks safe, but his knight takes your bishop first. } 12. Nxd6 Qxd6 13. d4 { Slightly better for him: he got your good bishop for his knight, and your lead in time is gone. [%csl Rd6] }) (11... Bc7 { The bishop steps back, and now his knight takes yours on the edge. } 12. Nxa5 Bxa5 13. O-O { Level: a pair of knights came off and he castled, so your lead has melted away. [%csl Ra5] }) (11... Bf5 { The bishop develops, but his knight takes your other bishop with check. } 12. Nxd6+ Qxd6 13. d4 { About level: he swapped his wandering knight for your best bishop. [%csl Rd6] }) (11... Bc5 { The bishop dodges, but his knight gets out of trouble with tempo. } 12. Ne3 O-O 13. O-O { About level: his knight found a safe home on e3, and he castled. [%csl Re3] }) (11... Be6 { The bishop defends your knight, but his knight takes on d6 with check. } 12. Nxd6+ Qxd6 13. d3 { Better for him: your pair of bishops is gone and he is still a pawn up. [%csl Rd6] }) (11... c5 { The pawn guards nothing important, and his knight takes your bishop with check. } 12. Nxd6+ Qxd6 { Better for him: your good bishop is gone, and you are still a pawn down. [%csl Rd6] }) (11... e3 { The pawn lunges, but his knight takes your bishop first. } 12. Nxd6+ Qxd6 13. dxe3 { Much better for him: he took your bishop and then your pawn. [%csl Re3] }) (11... Bg4 { The bishop hits his queen, but his knight takes on d6 with check first. } 12. Nxd6+ Qxd6 13. Bxg4 { Lost for you: both your bishops are gone for his knight. [%csl Rg4] }) (11... Bb7 { The bishop takes a blocked diagonal, and his knight takes your bishop on d6 with check. } 12. Nxd6+ Qxd6 { Better for him: your good bishop is gone, and he is a pawn up. [%csl Rd6] }) (11... Bd7 { The bishop steps to d7 and blocks your own queen, so his knight takes on d6 for free. } 12. Nxd6+ Ke7 { Lost for you: your bishop is gone, and your king had to walk. [%csl Re7] }) 12. Bxc4 { His bishop takes back on c4, its fourth move of the game. What is the quickest way to finish getting ready? } 12... O-O { Castled. Your king is safe, and his still sits in the middle. Against pawn h3, your rook comes to e8 just the same. } (12... Qa5 { Also strong: your queen comes to a5 and pins his d pawn to his king. } 13. Nc3 { Much the same: your queen stands active on a5, and castling can wait a move. [%csl Ga5] }) (12... Rb8 { Just as good: the rook takes the b column before you castle. } 13. h3 O-O 14. d4 { Still slightly better for you: he shored up his centre while your king waited. [%csl Rd4] }) (12... Qc7 { The queen lines up with the bishop, but that gives him a free move. } 13. h3 O-O 14. Nc3 { About level: he stopped any trick on h2 and brought his knight out. [%csl Rh3] }) (12... Bg4 { The bishop chases nothing: he simply steps back and your king is still in the centre. } 13. Be2 Be6 14. d4 { Level: his pieces came back into play and your lead is gone. [%csl Rd4] }) (12... Ng4 { The knight jumps forward, but his king is not the target yet. } 13. Nc3 O-O 14. Nxe4 { About level: his knight grabbed your pawn on e4, and your attack has not started. [%csl Re4] }) (12... Qe7 { The queen guards your e pawn, but it blocks your bishop's retreat. } 13. Nc3 O-O 14. h3 { Slightly better for you, though castling first was stronger. [%csl Be7] }) (12... Bf5 { The bishop develops, but your king stays in the middle one move too long. } 13. d4 Rb8 14. Nc3 { About level: he used the time to push his d pawn and bring his knight out. [%csl Rd4] }) (12... e3 { The pawn lunges, but his d pawn takes it. } 13. dxe3 O-O { Slightly better for him: two pawns up, though your pieces are active. [%csl Re3] }) (12... Be6 { The bishop offers a trade that splits your pawns. } 13. Bxe6 fxe6 14. Qe2 { Slightly better for him: your pawns are split, and his queen eyes your pawn on e4. [%csl Re6] }) (12... c5 { The pawn grabs space, but it lets his bishop check on b5 and swap itself off. } 13. Bb5+ Bd7 14. Bxd7+ Qxd7 { Level: the bishops came off, and your lead has shrunk. [%csl Rd7] }) (12... Bd7 { The bishop develops, but it does nothing on d7. } 13. Nc3 O-O 14. d3 { About level: he untangled while your king waited. [%csl Rd7] }) (12... Bb7 { The bishop takes a diagonal your c pawn blocks, and your king waits. } 13. Nc3 O-O { Level: your lead melted while the bishop took a quiet square. [%csl Rb7] }) 13. Nc3 { He brings out his knight and keeps his king in the middle for now: castling would walk into a bishop sacrifice on h2. What does your rook want? } 13... Re8 { The rook backs up your pawn on e4 from behind. You are castled with a rook in play, while his king still sits on e1 and his queen bishop has never moved. A pawn down, and well ahead in time: that is the Polerio. } (13... Qa5 { Just as good: your queen hits the bishop and joins the game. } 14. Be2 { Just as strong as the rook move: your queen is active, and your rook can still come to e8. [%csl Ga5] }) (13... Rb8 { Just as good: the rook takes the b column instead. } 14. d3 exd3 15. O-O dxc2 16. Qxc2 { Still slightly better for you: the rook on b8 presses on his queen side. [%csl Gb8] }) (13... Bf5 { Just as good: the bishop develops first. } 14. d4 exd3 15. Bxd3 { Still slightly better for you: a pair of bishops can come off, and your rook goes to e8 next. [%csl Gf5] }) (13... Bg4 { The bishop hits nothing important, and he gets a free move. } 14. Be2 Bf5 15. O-O { About level: your bishop went there and back, and his king reached safety. [%csl Rg1] }) (13... Qc7 { The queen aims at h2, but he answers with a small pawn move. } 14. h3 Qa5 15. Qe2 { About level: your queen walked twice, and he consolidated. [%csl Rh3] }) (13... Qe7 { The queen guards e4, but she gets in the way. [%cal Ge7e4] } 14. h3 Re8 15. Qe2 { About level: his queen and bishop cover his king, and your lead is shrinking. [%csl Re2] }) (13... c5 { The pawn grabs space, but it lets him open the centre. } 14. d3 exd3 15. O-O dxc2 16. Qxc2 { Level: the centre opened and he castled, so your lead has gone. [%csl Rg1] }) (13... Be6 { The bishop offers a trade that leaves you with split pawns. } 14. Bxe6 fxe6 15. Qe2 { Slightly better for him: your pawn on e4 is weak, and his queen hits it. [%csl Re4] }) (13... e3 { The pawn push gives a pawn and opens nothing. } 14. dxe3 Re8 15. h3 { Slightly better for him: two pawns up, and he closed the door on h2. [%csl Re3] }) (13... Bd7 { The bishop develops quietly, but he opens the centre. } 14. d3 { About level: he gets to swap off your pawn on e4. [%csl Re4] }) (13... Bb7 { The bishop heads for a blocked diagonal. } 14. Qe2 { Level: his queen covers his king side, and your lead is gone. [%csl Re2] }) 0-1


