{ Back to the main line: your bishop on d6 is hitting his knight on e5, and his king has not castled. }
11. f4 { He props his knight up with the f pawn this time. It is playable, but it loosens the pawns around his king. How do you make him pay? [%csl Re1] } 11... exf3 { En passant: your pawn takes on f3 as if his pawn had moved one square, and the lines toward his king open. } (11... O-O { Castling is natural, but his pawns stay where they are and his knight on e5 stands firm. } 12. O-O Nd5 13. d4 exd3 14. cxd3 { About level: he castled first, and your chance to hit his bare king has gone. [%csl Rg1] }) (11... Qb6 { The queen eyes his king, but she opens nothing. } 12. Nc3 O-O 13. Na4 { About level: his knight chases your queen, and he castles next. [%csl Ra4] }) (11... Qc7 { The queen adds a second attacker to his knight, but he just castles. } 12. O-O O-O 13. Nc3 { About level: his king got away in time, and the knight on e5 still stands. [%csl Re5] }) (11... Nd5 { The knight heads for the middle, but his king escapes first. } 12. O-O O-O 13. d4 { About level: he castled and closed the centre. [%csl Rd4] }) (11... Bf5 { The bishop develops, but his pawn on f4 still guards the knight. } 12. O-O O-O 13. Nc3 { About level: he castled, and the lines toward his king never opened. [%csl Rf4] }) (11... Bxe5 { You take the knight, but his pawn takes back and your bishop is gone. } 12. fxe5 Ng4 13. d4 { Slightly better for him: his pawns hold the centre, and you gave up your good bishop. [%csl Re5] }) (11... Be6 { The bishop develops, but taking en passant is possible only right now. } 12. Nc3 O-O 13. O-O { About level: he castled, and his pawn on f4 still props up the knight. [%csl Rf4] }) (11... c5 { The pawn push invites a bishop check on b5 first. } 12. Bb5+ Nd7 13. d4 exd3 14. Qxd3 { Slightly better for him: the centre opened while your pieces were tangled. [%csl Rd7] }) (11... e3 { The pawn lunges forward and is simply taken by his d pawn. } 12. dxe3 O-O 13. O-O { Better for him: two pawns up, and castled. [%csl Re3] }) (11... Bg4 { The bishop hits his bishop, but his knight takes it. } 12. Nxg4 Nxg4 13. Bxg4 { Lost for you: a whole piece down. [%csl Rg4] }) (11... Bb7 { The bishop takes a blocked diagonal, and your pawn misses its chance on f3. } 12. d4 exd3 13. Qxd3 { Slightly better for him: the centre opened while his f pawn held the knight. [%csl Rf4] }) (11... Bd7 { The bishop comes to d7, and the chance on f3 passes. } 12. d4 exd3 13. Qxd3 { Slightly better for him: his centre opened on his terms. [%csl Rd3] }) (11... Ba6 { The bishop comes to a6, where his bishop takes it. } 12. Bxa6 { Much better for him: he just won your bishop. [%csl Ra6] }) 12. Nxf3 { His knight takes back on f3. His king is still on e1, and his e pawn and f pawn are both gone. } 12... O-O { Castled: three of your pieces are out and your king is safe, against his two, and his king sits on e1 with his e pawn and f pawn gone. That is what the Polerio's lead in time buys. } (12... Bb7 { Just as good: the bishop takes the long diagonal first. [%csl Gb7] } 13. d4 O-O 14. Nc3 { Much the same: you castled a move later, and your bishop eyes his king side. [%csl Gb7] }) (12... Qb6 { Just as good: the queen hits b2 and keeps him from castling for a moment. } 13. d4 c5 14. O-O { Much the same: sharp play on both sides, and his king is the more exposed one. [%csl Gb6] }) (12... Bg4 { Also fine: the bishop hits his knight on f3, with his bishop right behind it. } 13. O-O { Level: he castled first, and the game is a little calmer. [%csl Gg4] }) (12... Rb8 { Also fine: the rook takes the b column. } 13. O-O { Level: he castled, and your rook took the open b column. [%csl Gb8] }) (12... Qc7 { The queen aims at h2, but he castles out of the way. } 13. Nc3 O-O 14. O-O { About level: his king reached safety before your queen could do anything. [%csl Rg1] }) (12... Ng4 { The knight jumps forward too early. } 13. O-O O-O 14. Nc3 { About level: he castled, and your knight on g4 hits nothing. [%csl Rg4] }) (12... Bf5 { The bishop develops, but castling first kept more pressure. } 13. Nc3 O-O 14. O-O { Level: both kings are safe, a quieter game. [%csl Bf5] }) (12... c5 { The pawn grabs space, but his bishop checks first. } 13. Bb5+ Bd7 14. Qe2+ Be7 { About level: his checks bought him time to untangle. [%csl Rb5] }) (12... Bd7 { The bishop develops quietly, and his king castles first. } 13. O-O { Level: he got castled before your pieces got going. [%csl Rg1] }) 0-1


[Event "Polerio Defence: answer the bishop grab on d5"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/NMtH8AT9/LbfhSgRm"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n2n2/3pp1N1/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2n2n2/3pp1N1/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R_w_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ Your pawn on d5 has just blocked his bishop's view of f7. }
5. Bxd5 { His bishop grabs your pawn on d5, hoping that after the swaps his pawn will hit your knight. It is a mistake: his knight on g5 has no guard. Take the bishop first. } 5... Nxd5 { Your knight takes the bishop. If his knight grabs f7 now, your king takes it. } (5... Na5 { The Polerio knight jump, but his bishop no longer sits on c4, and his knight is free to take f7. } 6. Nxf7 Qe7 7. Nxh8 { Lost for you: the knight on f7 hit queen and rook together, and your rook in the corner is gone. [%csl Rh8] }) (5... Bg4 { The bishop hits his queen, but his bishop takes f7 with check. } 6. Bxf7+ Ke7 7. f3 { Better for him: your king is stuck in the middle, and his bishop sits on f7. [%csl Rf7] }) (5... Nd4 { The knight jumps forward, but his bishop takes f7 with check. } 6. Bxf7+ Ke7 7. Bb3 { Much better for him: two pawns up, and your king has lost the right to castle. [%csl Re7] }) (5... Be6 { The bishop blocks, but his knight swaps it off and f7 is weak. } 6. Nxe6 fxe6 7. Bxc6+ { Much better for him: he won a pawn and wrecked your pawns. [%csl Re6] }) (5... Qxd5 { Taking back with the queen drops her to his pawn. } 6. exd5 { Lost for you: his pawn took your queen. [%csl Rd5] }) 6. exd5 { His pawn takes back and hits your knight. Two of his men are loose now, the knight on g5 and the pawn on d5. Which one do you take? } 6... Qxg5 { The queen takes the knight on g5, the bigger prize, and she now eyes his pawn on g2. } (6... Qxd5 { The pawn is the smaller prize, and his knight gets away. } 7. O-O Bf5 8. Nc3 { Better for you, but only a pawn up: his knight on g5 was the piece worth taking. [%csl Gg5] }) (6... Nd4 { Saving the knight is natural, but his knight on g5 escapes. } 7. d3 h6 8. Nf3 { Better for you, but only a little: the knight you could have taken ran home. [%csl Gg5] }) (6... Nb4 { The knight runs, and his knight on g5 runs too. } 7. d3 Be7 8. Nf3 { Slightly better for you, a tiny edge where a whole piece was waiting. [%csl Gg5] }) (6... Bb4 { The bishop comes out, but his pawn takes your knight first. } 7. dxc6 Qxg5 { Level: the pieces are even again, but taking on g5 at once was much stronger. [%csl Rc6] }) (6... Bc5 { The bishop eyes f2, but his pawn takes your knight. } 7. dxc6 Qxg5 { Level: the pieces are even, and the big chance has passed. [%csl Rc6] }) (6... e4 { The pawn push hits nothing that matters, and he guards his knight in time. } 7. d3 Qxd5 { About level: you got his d pawn, but his knight on g5 is safe. [%csl Gg5] }) 7. dxc6 { His pawn takes your knight, so you are a pawn behind for the moment, and his pawn on c6 hits b7. How do you come out ahead? } 7... Qxg2 { Your queen takes g2 and hits his rook on h1. Now watch. [%tail] } (7... bxc6 { Taking back the pawn is natural, but his king side stays whole. } 8. O-O Bd6 9. d3 { Level: material is even, and the chance to wreck his king side has gone. [%csl Gg2] }) (7... e4 { The pawn push threatens nothing big, and he castles. } 8. O-O Bh3 9. g3 { Slightly better for you, but his king got to safety. [%csl Rg1] }) (7... Bd6 { Development, but his pawn on c6 is still alive. } 8. Qf3 Bg4 9. Qe3 { Level: his queen came out, and the pawn on g2 stayed in place. [%csl Gg2] }) (7... Bb4 { The bishop comes out, but his queen jumps to f3 in time. } 8. Qf3 { Level: his queen covers his king side, and his pawn on c6 still hits b7. [%cal Rf3g2] }) (7... Bc5 { The bishop hits f2, but his queen comes to f3 in time. } 8. Qf3 { Level: g2 is covered, and the chance to smash his king side is gone. [%cal Rf3g2] }) 8. Rf1 Bh3 9. Qe2 bxc6 10. d3 Qxf1+ 11. Qxf1 Bxf1 12. Kxf1 Bd6 { You are the exchange and a pawn up: two rooks against his rook and knight, and his king can never castle. Taking the knight on g5 first did it. [%csl Gh8,Ga8] } 0-1


