{ Your king pawn has chased his knight a second time, and his bishop is still out on a4. }
10. Ne5 { His knight jumps forward to e5 and looks proud there. It is a mistake, though. Can you see why? } 10... Qd4 { A fork: your queen hits the knight on e5 and his bishop at once, and whichever he saves, you take the other. Queen to e2 was his move. } (10... Bd6 { The bishop hits the knight, but he guards it with a pawn. } 11. d4 O-O 12. O-O { About level, when a whole piece was on offer. [%csl Ga4] }) (10... Qc7 { The queen hits the knight, but she hits only one piece. } 11. d4 Bd6 12. O-O { Level: he guarded the knight and castled. [%csl Ga4] }) (10... Qd5 { The queen hits the knight, but his pawn to d4 guards it and drives her back. } 11. d4 exd3 12. O-O { Better for him: your queen stands in the open, and he castled. [%csl Rd5] }) (10... Bb7 { The bishop guards c6, but his knight hops away. [%cal Gb7c6] } 11. Ng4 { About level: his knight jumped out of danger, and the queen fork never came. [%csl Rg4] }) (10... Bc5 { The bishop develops, but his knight takes on c6 and hits your queen. } 11. Nxc6 Qd7 12. d4 { About level: his knight grabbed a pawn and got away, and the fork never came. [%csl Rc6] }) (10... Be6 { The bishop develops, but his pawn to d4 guards the knight. } 11. d4 Qc7 12. O-O { About level: his knight on e5 is guarded, when a whole piece was on offer. [%csl Re5] }) (10... Ba6 { The bishop stops him castling, but his pawn guards the knight. } 11. d4 { Slightly better for him: the knight on e5 is guarded now, and a whole piece slipped away. [%csl Re5] }) (10... Bb4 { The bishop comes out, but his knight takes on c6 and hits your queen. } 11. Nxc6 Qd7 12. Nxb4 Qxa4 { Better for him: a pair of bishops came off, and he is two pawns up. [%csl Rb4] }) (10... Be7 { The bishop develops, but his knight takes on c6. } 11. Nxc6 Nxc6 12. Bxc6+ Bd7 13. Bxa8 Qxa8 { Better for him: he won a rook for a bishop, and he is two pawns up. [%csl Ra8] }) (10... Bg4 { The bishop hits his queen, but his knight simply takes it. } 11. Nxg4 { Lost for you: his knight took your bishop, and taking back drops your knight to his queen. [%csl Rg4] }) 11. Bxc6+ { He gives the bishop for your pawn with check, as most players do. His best was pawn to f4, guarding the knight, but the bishop falls anyway; pawn to b4 saves nothing either. Take back. } 11... Nxc6 { Your knight takes back on c6. } (11... Bd7 { Blocking with the bishop lets him swap off and keep his knight. } 12. Bxd7+ Nxd7 13. Nxd7 Qxd7 { Much better for him: the fork came to nothing, and you are two pawns down. [%csl Rd7] }) 12. Nxc6 { His knight takes your knight on c6 and looks safe for the moment. Is it? } 12... Qc5 { Your queen hits the knight on c6, and every square it could run to is covered: the knight is trapped. } (12... Qd5 { Just as good: from d5 your queen also hits the knight and cuts off its escape. } 13. Nxa7 Rxa7 { Much better for you: the knight took a pawn on its way out and still died. [%csl Ga7] }) (12... Qd6 { The queen hits the knight, but it has a way out. } 13. Na5 Bg4 14. f3 { Much better for you, but his knight escaped to a5. [%csl Ra5] }) (12... Qb6 { The queen hits the knight, but it runs back to e5. } 13. Ne5 Qd4 14. f4 { Slightly better for you: his knight got back to e5 in one piece. [%csl Re5] }) (12... Bg4 { The bishop hits his queen, but his knight takes yours on d4. } 13. Nxd4 Bxd1 14. Kxd1 { Lost for you: the queens came off, and your bishop went with them. [%csl Rd1] }) (12... Bc5 { The bishop comes out, but his knight on c6 hits your queen. } 13. Nxd4 Bxd4 { Lost for you: your queen went for his knight. [%csl Rd4] }) (12... Be7 { The bishop steps out, but your queen is still hit by his knight. } 13. Nxd4 { Lost for you: his knight took your queen for free. [%csl Rd4] }) 13. Nxa7 { The knight grabs a pawn on its way out, the choice of most players. Pawn to d4 first was his best, to hit your queen. } 13... Qxa7 { Your queen takes the knight. You have a whole piece for his three pawns, his queen side has not moved, and your queen on a7 stares at f2. You are much better. } (13... Rxa7 { Also winning: the rook takes the knight instead, a touch less strong. } 14. Nc3 { Much better for you: a piece for three pawns, and your pieces are the livelier ones. [%csl Ga7] }) (13... Bg4 { Just as good: the bishop hits his queen first, and the knight on a7 is not going anywhere. } 14. f3 exf3 15. gxf3 Be6 { Much better for you: his king side is torn, and the knight on a7 is still trapped. [%csl Ga7] }) (13... Be6 { Also winning, but it lets his queen come out first. } 14. Qe2 Qxa7 { Much better for you: you took the knight a move later. [%csl Ga7] }) 0-1


