{ Your rook has just left the long diagonal, and with your pawn it now hits his bishop: the Polerio chase goes on. [%cal Gb8b5] }
9. Bxc6+ { He grabs a second pawn with check, as most club players do. It is a mistake: his bishop should have stepped back to d3. Take back first. } 9... Nxc6 { Your knight from a5 takes back. } (9... Bd7 { Blocking with the bishop lets his bishop take on f7 later. } 10. Bd5 Qc7 11. Bxf7+ Ke7 { Much better for him: three pawns up, and your king is stuck in the middle. [%csl Re7] }) (9... Nd7 { Blocking with the other knight leaves f7 without a guard. } 10. Qxf7# { Checkmate: his queen took f7, and your king had no square. [%csl Rf7] }) (9... Qd7 { The queen blocks, but his bishop just takes her. } 10. Bxd7+ Bxd7 { Lost for you: your queen is gone for a bishop. [%csl Rd7] }) (9... Ke7 { The king steps up, and his queen checks it at once. } 10. Qa3+ Qd6 11. Qxd6+ Kxd6 { Much better for him: the queens are gone, and he is two pawns up. [%csl Rd6] }) 10. Qxc6+ { His queen takes back with check, and he is two pawns up. How do you meet the check? } 10... Nd7 { Right: the knight blocks, so your light bishop stays free to join in from b7 or a6, and his queen stands alone in your half. } (10... Bd7 { The bishop blocks, but it gives up its diagonal, and his queen escapes to c4. } 11. Qc4 Qe7 12. Nc3 { Slightly better for him: two pawns up, and your bishop is stuck on d7. [%csl Rd7] }) (10... Qd7 { The queen offers a swap, but that suits the side with extra pawns. } 11. Qxd7+ Nxd7 12. d3 { Better for him: the queens are gone, and he is two pawns up. [%csl Rd7] }) (10... Ke7 { The king steps up, but his queen checks again and takes your pawn. } 11. Qc5+ Ke8 12. Qxe5+ Qe7 13. Qxe7+ Bxe7 { Much better for him: the queens came off, and he is three pawns up. [%csl Re5] }) 11. d3 { He opens a road for his other bishop, which now guards his knight. Castling or a queen move would have left the knight hanging to your queen; knight back to f3 or e4 changes little. What is your most useful move now? } 11... Be7 { The bishop hits his knight on g5 and clears the way to castle. } (11... h6 { Kicking the knight is natural, but it simply steps back to f3. } 12. Nf3 Bc5 13. Qe4 { Level: his queen got back to safety, and your lead has shrunk. [%csl Re4] }) (11... Bc5 { The bishop hits f2, but it does nothing to his knight. } 12. Qf3 O-O 13. Nc3 { Level: his queen went home, and your lead is gone. [%csl Rf3] }) (11... Bb4+ { The check only helps his knight come out. } 12. Nc3 O-O 13. Qe4 { Level: his knight came out, and his queen found safety. [%csl Re4] }) (11... Bb7 { The bishop hits his queen, but she steps to c4 and your bishop bites on nothing. } 12. Qc4 Qf6 13. Nc3 { Better for him: two pawns up, with his pieces coming out. [%csl Rc4] }) (11... Rb6 { The rook hits his queen, but she steps to c4 and hits f7 together with his knight. } 12. Qc4 Rf6 13. Nc3 { Better for him: your rook had to guard f7, and he is two pawns up. [%cal Rf6f7] }) (11... Rb4 { The rook swings out, but his queen just steps back. } 12. Qf3 Nf6 13. O-O { Better for him: two pawns up, and his king is safe. [%csl Rg1] }) (11... Bd6 { The bishop steps where his queen can take it. } 12. Qxd6 { Lost for you: nothing guarded d6, and your bishop simply dropped off. [%csl Rd6] }) (11... e4 { The pawn push opens nothing and drops another pawn. } 12. Qd5 Qf6 13. Nxe4 { Much better for him: three pawns up now. [%csl Re4] }) 12. Ne4 { His knight backs off to e4, where most players put it; on f3 you would simply castle. What now? } 12... Rb6 { The rook hits his queen, and she has to run again. Two pawns down, you have the better game: you are ready to castle, and his queen side has not moved. [%cal Gb6c6] } (12... O-O { Castling is natural, but it gives his knight time to jump to d6. } 13. Nd6 { Level: his knight jumped to d6, and the chance to chase his queen has passed. [%csl Rd6] }) (12... Bb7 { The bishop hits his queen, but his knight checks on d6 first. } 13. Nd6+ Bxd6 14. Qxd6 { Slightly better for him: he swapped his knight for your bishop and kept both extra pawns. [%csl Rd6] }) (12... f5 { The pawn hits his knight, but it jumps to d6 with check. } 13. Nd6+ Bxd6 14. Qxd6 { Better for him: two pawns up, and your king side is loose. [%csl Rf5] }) (12... h6 { The pawn move is too slow: his knight checks on d6. } 13. Nd6+ Bxd6 14. Qxd6 { Better for him: his queen sits in the middle of your camp, and he is two pawns up. [%csl Rd6] }) (12... Bb4+ { The check only helps his bishop come out. } 13. Bd2 O-O 14. Bxb4 Rxb4 { Better for him: the bishops came off, and he is still two pawns up. [%csl Rd2] }) (12... Rb4 { The rook swings out, but his knight checks on d6. } 13. Nd6+ Bxd6 14. Qxd6 { Better for him: your rook stands out of play on b4, and your bishop is gone. [%csl Rb4] }) 0-1


