{ Your rook has just hit his queen, and she has to find a new square. If she goes to a4, as most players choose, your f pawn kicks his knight. }
13. Qc3 { His queen steps to c3, as one player in five does here. It is a blunder: queen to c4 was his move. Can you punish it? } 13... Bb4 { Right: the bishop pins his queen to his king. She cannot step off the line, so she is lost for a bishop. [%cal Gb4e1] } (13... O-O { Castling is natural, but his king castles too and leaves the line. } 14. O-O f5 15. Ng3 { Better for you, but his king got off the diagonal, and the chance to win his queen is gone. [%csl Rg1] }) 14. Nd6+ { His knight checks first, the most common try: castling or guarding her with his bishop would lose her at once. How do you meet the check? } 14... Kf8 { The king steps aside, and his queen is still pinned. } (14... Ke7 { Just as good: the king steps up, and the pin still holds. } 15. Nc4 Bxc3+ 16. Nxc3 { Much better for you: you won his queen for a bishop. [%csl Gc3] }) (14... Bxd6 { Also winning: your bishop takes the knight instead and lets the queen go. } 15. O-O O-O { Much better for you: you won his knight for nothing. [%csl Gd6] }) (14... Rxd6 { The rook takes the knight, but that lets his queen take your bishop. } 15. Qxb4 { Better for him: his queen escaped and took your bishop. [%csl Rb4] }) 15. Qxb4 { His queen takes your bishop, since any other move lets your bishop take her. } 15... Rxb4 { Your rook takes his queen. A queen for a bishop: you are winning. [%csl Gb4] } 0-1


