{ Your knight has just landed on f4, hitting his bishop on d3. }
11. Be4 { His bishop jumps to the middle and eyes your pawn on c6. It is a mistake: rook to e1 was the move. Can you punish it? } 11... f5 { The pawn hits the bishop, and it has no good square left. } (11... O-O { Castling is calm, but it lets his bishop stay in the middle. } 12. d4 f5 13. Bxf4 { About level: he swapped his bishop for your knight and kept his centre. [%csl Rf4] }) (11... Nxg2 { The knight grabs a pawn, but his pawn to d4 opens the centre. } 12. d4 f5 13. Bg5 { Slightly better for him: your knight wandered off, and his pieces are active. [%csl Rg2] }) (11... Rb8 { The rook takes the b column, but his pawn to d4 hits your centre. } 12. d4 exd4 13. Nxd4 { Slightly better for him: the centre opened while his bishop stayed on e4. [%csl Re4] }) (11... Ng6 { The knight steps back, and his bishop takes it. } 12. Bxg6 hxg6 13. Re1 { Slightly better for him: a pair of minor pieces came off, and your pawn on e5 is weak. [%csl Re5] }) (11... Bg4 { The bishop pins his knight, but his pawn to d4 breaks into your centre. } 12. d4 { Better for him: your pawn on e5 is under fire, and his bishop is safe. [%csl Re5] }) (11... Be6 { The bishop develops, but his pawn to d4 hits your centre. } 12. d4 { Better for him: your pawn on e5 falls, and his bishop stays in the middle. [%csl Re5] }) (11... Ba6 { The bishop stops nothing, and his rook comes to e1. } 12. Re1 { Better for him: his bishop is safe on e4, and your pawn on e5 is under fire. [%csl Re5] }) (11... c5 { The pawn opens the long diagonal to your own rook. [%cal Re4a8] } 12. d4 { Lost for you: his bishop hits your rook in the corner, and your centre crumbles. [%csl Ra8] }) 12. Bd3 { The bishop drops back to d3, the choice of most players. Knight to c3, giving the bishop for two pawns, was his best. Now finish the job. } 12... e4 { The pawn forks his bishop and his knight. } (12... O-O { Also strong: you castle first and keep the fork in reserve. } 13. Be2 { Better for you, but his bishop slipped away, and the fork was quicker. [%csl Ge4] }) (12... Nxd3 { You take the bishop, but that lets him off the hook. } 13. cxd3 c5 14. d4 { Slightly better for you, but you let a whole piece go. [%csl Ge4] }) (12... Nxg2 { The knight grabs a pawn, but his king takes it. } 13. Kxg2 e4 14. b4 { Slightly better for you, a long way short of winning a piece. [%csl Rg2] }) (12... Qf6 { The queen comes out, but his knight gets time to escape. } 13. Nc3 O-O 14. Be2 { Slightly better for you, when a whole piece was on offer. [%csl Rf6] }) (12... Be6 { The bishop develops, but his rook comes to e1 first. } 13. Re1 { About level, when pushing the pawn at once won a piece. [%csl Ge4] }) (12... Bd7 { The bishop comes to d7, and his pawn hits your knight. } 13. b4 { About level: his pawn hit your knight on a5 first. [%csl Ra5] }) (12... c5 { The pawn goes to c5, and his bishop jumps out with a check. } 13. Bb5+ Kf7 { Better for him: your king had to step up, and the fork never came. [%csl Rf7] }) 13. Re1 { His rook pins your pawn to your king, so it cannot take anything yet. Pawn to b4 was his better try. What now? } 13... O-O { Castled: your king steps off the e column, and your pawn is free to take again. Now watch. [%tail] } (13... Ne6 { The knight gets out of the way, but his bishop takes your pawn. } 14. Bxe4 fxe4 15. Rxe4 { Better for you, but he got two pawns for the bishop, and your king is still in the middle. [%csl Re8] }) (13... Nxg2 { The knight takes on g2, and his king takes it back. } 14. Kxg2 O-O 15. b4 { Better for you, but your knight was traded for a pawn. [%csl Rg2] }) (13... Kf7 { The king steps off the column by hand, and loses the right to castle. } 14. Bf1 exf3 15. Qxf3 { Better for you, but castling would have kept your king safer. [%csl Rf7] }) (13... Be6 { The bishop breaks the pin, but his knight escapes to d4. } 14. Nd4 { Slightly better for you, but the knight got away. [%csl Rd4] }) (13... c5 { The pawn push lets his bishop out with a check. } 14. Bb5+ Kf7 { About level: your king had to walk, and his knight slipped away. [%csl Rf7] }) (13... e3 { The pawn pushes on, but his rook takes it with check. } 14. Rxe3+ Be7 { Much better for him: the pawn fell with check, and the fork is gone. [%csl Re3] }) 14. Bf1 exf3 15. Qxf3 Qh4 { A knight for two pawns: your pawns chased his bishop all the way home and then took his knight. That is the Polerio chase at its best. } 0-1


