is g pawn. Now watch. [%tail] } (12... Bb6 { The bishop steps out of danger, but it leaves his knight alone. } 13. d3 { Better for you, but less: his pawn move lets his bishop come out and hit your queen. [%csl Rc1] }) (12... O-O { Castling gives him time to hit your queen. } 13. d3 { Slightly better for you: his knight on h3 still guards f2, and his bishop is ready to come out. [%cal Rh3f2] }) (12... Be6 { The bishop develops, but his king castles out of reach. } 13. O-O Bxh3 14. gxh3 { About level: he castled first, so taking on h3 won nothing. [%csl Rg1] }) (12... Bf5 { The bishop develops, but his king castles. } 13. O-O { Level: he castled, and his knight on h3 is safe now. [%csl Rh3] }) (12... Bd7 { The bishop develops, but his knight on h3 stays. } 13. d3 { Level: the knight on h3 guards f2, and his bishop is free to come out. [%cal Rh3f2] }) (12... e3 { The pawn lunges, but his f pawn takes it and f2 is gone as a target. } 13. fxe3 { Much better for him: your pawn is gone, and so is the target on f2. [%csl Re3] }) (12... Bg4 { The bishop goes to g4, but his bishop on e2 just takes it. } 13. Bxg4 { Much better for him: he took your bishop for nothing. [%csl Rg4] }) 13. O-O Be6 { A knight up for a pawn: his knight on h3 was the only guard of f2, and your bishop took it before he castled. } 0-1


