13. O-O { He castles too, like most players here. It is a blunder: knight to c3 first was the move. Can you punish it? } 13... Bxh2+ { Your bishop gives itself up on h2 with check, to drag his king out into your knight's reach. If his king steps into the corner instead, your knight jumps in all the same. } (13... Ng4 { Also strong: the knight hits h2, and he has to defend. } 14. h3 Bh2+ 15. Kh1 { Much better for you as well, but the bishop sacrifice is stronger still: it wins his queen. [%csl Gh2] }) (13... Qc7 { The queen aims at h2, but he simply guards it. } 14. h3 Qe7 15. d4 { Slightly better for you, but the chance for the sacrifice on h2 has gone. [%csl Gh2] }) (13... Bg4 { The bishop hits his queen, but it steps away. } 14. Be2 Qd7 15. d4 { Slightly better for you, but his king side is closed now. [%csl Gh2] }) (13... Re8 { The rook comes to the e column, but his king gets a breather. } 14. d4 Bf5 15. Nc3 { Slightly better for you, but the sacrifice on h2 was much stronger. [%csl Gh2] }) 14. Kxh2 { His king takes the bishop and stands on h2. Do not let him breathe. } 14... Ng4+ { Check from the knight, and your queen is ready to join in. Now watch. [%tail] } (14... Qd6+ { Check, but his king runs back to g1 and the attack fizzles. } 15. Kg1 Ng4 16. g3 { Level: your bishop is gone for a pawn, and his king found shelter behind the pawn on g3. [%csl Rg3] }) (14... Qc7+ { Check, but his king steps back and you are a bishop down. } 15. Kg1 Ng4 16. g3 { Better for him: you gave a bishop and got a pawn. [%csl Rg3] }) 15. Kg3 { His king steps up to g3, as most players do. Back to g1 was his best. } 15... Qg5 16. Qxg4 Bxg4 { Your bishop takes his queen. You gave a bishop and a knight and got his queen and a pawn: a sacrifice on h2 that pays. } 0-1


